WEBVTT

00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:04.950
NIST PQC standards provide a foundation for migrating away from public-key cryptography

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.080
that is vulnerable to future quantum attack.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.420
The standards give organizations shared terminology and concrete algorithm references,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.880
but they do not perform the migration by themselves.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.180
Security teams still need inventory, planning, testing, vendor engagement,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.870
and operational discipline.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.520
FIPS 203 covers ML-KEM for key establishment.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.600
FIPS 204 covers ML-DSA for digital signatures.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.360
FIPS 205 covers SLH-DSA for stateless hash-based digital signatures.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.440
Each standard addresses a different role in cryptographic systems.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.560
Key establishment protects how parties create shared secrets.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.255
Digital signatures protect authenticity, integrity, and trust relationships.

00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:42.570
The operational migration questions are different for each role.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.245
Additional algorithms such as Falcon and HQC matter for diversity, backup options,

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.430
and future planning.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:51.205
They remind us that PQC migration is not a one-time naming exercise.

00:00:51.205 --> 00:00:54.700
The standards ecosystem will continue maturing through additional documents,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.140
protocol integration, validation, product support, and field experience.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.480
Organizations should avoid locking themselves into brittle assumptions.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.800
The goal is not merely to know algorithm names.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.555
The goal is to connect standards to real systems: where cryptography exists,

00:01:07.555 --> 00:01:11.450
what data or trust depends on it, which vendors control it, which protocols carry it,

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.145
how it will be tested, and how it can be changed later.

00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:18.680
PQC readiness is the combination of standards awareness and practical migration execution.
